+# Agent Note: Document preview bundles the PDF.js legacy build for pre-ES2025 browsers
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-10-pdfjs-legacy-build.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Any fresh Web load after the document-preview plugin joined the roster failed on browsers without ES2025 `Iterator` helpers with `Failed to load plugins` / `failed to import loader entry ... (@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-documentpreview): Iterator is not defined`. The bundled `pdfjs-dist@6.3.289` modern build reads `Iterator.prototype` at import time and calls `Promise.try` from its worker bridge at runtime, both outside the repository's es2024 client target. One bad loader entry fails the whole plugin screen, so a phone on iOS < 18.4 or Chrome < 117 got a dead shell instead of a missing PDF tab. The failure looked vanilla-specific only because the reporter's full-mode tab predated the plugin's arrival and kept running its stale module graph; a fresh full-mode load failed identically.
+
+## Decision
+
+The preview now bundles `pdfjs-dist/legacy` (same 6.3.289 API, plus its maintained core-js polyfills) for both the main-thread runtime and the module worker: `runtime.ts` and the type-only imports reference `pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.min.mjs`, `assets.ts` and the `tsdown.config.ts` worker plugin reference `legacy/build/pdf.worker.min.mjs`, and `asset-imports.d.ts` re-exports the root entry's declarations for the minified legacy path (which ships no sibling types). The legacy main and worker builds were verified to import cleanly in a real Chromium with `globalThis.Iterator` deleted, and the legacy worker's core-js covers `Promise.try`, so old browsers get a working viewer rather than a failed shell.
+
+- **The real-library smoke already rendered through this build.** `pdf-smoke.client.spec.ts` imports `pdfjs-dist/legacy/build/pdf.mjs` with the legacy worker, so production now runs the exact module the smoke proves; the mock in `pdf-runtime.client.spec.ts` follows the new specifier.
+- **No hand-rolled platform polyfill.** A two-global `Iterator`/`Promise.try` shim was prototyped and verified, then removed in favor of the maintained build: the shim covered only the two APIs audited today, while the legacy build tracks every gap the dependency assumes.
+- **Bundle cost is ~110KB.** The minified legacy main and worker are each about 50–60KB larger than their modern twins on a ~7MB plugin artifact.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Keep the modern build and ship a compat module.** Rejected: it fixed the import and the audited runtime calls, but every future PDF.js minor could assume another ES2025 API and break old browsers again with the same dead shell. The maintained legacy build moves that tracking to upstream.
+
+**Pin PDF.js to a pre-Iterator major.** Rejected: the package's `BinaryDataFactory` asset plumbing and the `isEvalSupported`-era option comments target the v6 API, and a downgrade would trade a build-path change for an API migration with no offline way to verify the older line.
+
+**Tell affected users to update their browsers.** Rejected as the only answer: the failing class (system WebViews and iOS versions before 18.4) is exactly the phone audience the mobile layout work targets, and many of those browsers cannot be updated on demand.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Fresh loads on pre-ES2025 browsers render the shell and open PDFs; verified in a real Chromium with the `Iterator` global removed, for both the full and the vanilla profile compositions.
+- `pdfjs-dist` stays pinned at 6.3.289; a future major bump re-audits the modern-vs-legacy choice against the client's build target.
+- The package README records the legacy choice under Known Limitations.
