+# Agent Note: Web shell supports pre-119 browsers via compat shims and a ponyfilled boot tail
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-10-pre-119-browser-support.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+After the PDF.js legacy fix, old browsers still failed differently: a Chrome 111 page showed permanent "reconnecting" with no workspaces or models, plus `Promise.withResolvers is not a function` / `AbortSignal.any is not a function`. Client bundles call both APIs across the connection, session, and preview paths, but they are runtime APIs the es2024 build target does not polyfill (`withResolvers` needs Chrome 119+, `AbortSignal.any` Chrome 116+). The connection generation died during setup on those browsers. A further instance of the same class hid in the served index itself: the host-injected boot-readiness tail called `Promise.withResolvers()` in a classic script that runs before any shell module, so no module-level shim could cover it. Related: [PDF.js legacy build](2026-09-10-pdfjs-legacy-build.md) covers the `Iterator`/`Promise.try` gap for the preview dependency; this note covers the first-party gaps.
+
+## Decision
+
+- New `apps/web/src/compat.ts`, imported first in `main.ts`: installs `Promise.withResolvers` and `AbortSignal.any` only when missing, dependency-free, before every plugin entry evaluates. Verified bundled at the top of the shell entry.
+- `packages/host/webserver/src/injections.ts`: the `READY_MARKUP` tail builds the deferred inline (`var d = {}; d.promise = new Promise(...)`) instead of calling `Promise.withResolvers()`; the webserver spec asserts the new string.
+- No other shims: `Promise.try`/`Array.fromAsync`/`Object.groupBy` have no first-party callers, and `Array.at`/`Object.hasOwn`/`structuredClone` already exist on the supported floor (Chrome ~92+).
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Tell affected users to update.** Rejected: the household runs a Catalina-capped Chrome and older phones; several cannot move past Chrome 111.
+
+**Shim inside each calling package.** Rejected: the call sites span connection, gateway, workspace, and preview packages; one shell-level module covers them all including future callers, and the tail script needs its own inline form regardless.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Chrome 111 emulation (all four globals removed) loads sessions over the gateway with a stable WebSocket, zero reconnect text, and zero console errors; modern Chromium regressed clean.
+- `git pull` note: `compat.ts` is a new file (merge-immune); `main.ts` and `injections.ts` hunks are small and local. A post-merge hook rebuilds the web shell and the webserver lib (see `dsh-public-gateway-src/PROTECT.md` §5).
